---
import Docs from '../../../layouts/Docs.astro'
import { FeatureRow } from '@/components/kit'
import SeeAlso from '../../../components/SeeAlso.astro'
import SpecLinks from '../../../components/SpecLinks.astro'

// Camera & interaction — the concept page for how input drives the view.
// Built on the xAI kit + the Docs chrome (nav, TOC, prev/next), matching
// the sibling concept pages (globe, rtc, projections) so it slots into the
// Concepts sidebar group. Unlike globe.astro there is NO live React island
// here — the page is prose + a FeatureRow ledger + a "see it live" link.
//
// Honesty (REBUILD §6): every claim below is TRUE against the codebase —
// the projection-aware pan branch (Camera.pan, camera.ts:763), the orbit
// camera + ray↔sphere globe anchoring (globe-anchor.ts / globe.ts), the
// cursor-anchored zoomAt (camera.ts:856), bearing/pitch gestures with the
// disc pitch-lock (controller.ts / camera.ts:49), pan inertia + smooth
// wheel zoom (controller.ts:228 / :541), projection-aware unproject
// (camera.ts:710), and the Mapbox-parity camera API (camera-controller.ts).
// An ANIMATED fly-to camera is NOT shipped — easeTo/flyTo alias jumpTo
// (camera-controller.ts:281); that, plus the deferred azimuthal-disc
// screen→lon/lat unproject, is flagged ROADMAP.

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, '')
const repo = 'https://github.com/X-GIS/X-GIS'

// On-this-page TOC — matches the <section> ids below.
const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: 'the-model',   text: 'The model' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'orbit-pan',   text: 'Orbit the globe, pan the plane' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'zoom',        text: 'Zoom toward the cursor' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'rotate-tilt', text: 'Rotate and tilt' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'inertia',     text: 'Inertia' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'unproject',   text: 'Projection-aware unproject' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'api',         text: 'Programmatic control' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'roadmap',     text: 'Where it is going' },
]
---

<Docs
  current="concepts/camera"
  title="X-GIS — Camera & interaction"
  description="Projection-aware input: the same drag orbits the 3D globe and pans the flat plane, the cursor stays anchored across a zoom, flicks glide with inertia, and a Mapbox-parity camera API (setCenter / jumpTo / fitBounds / panBy) drives it programmatically."
  headings={headings}
>
  <header class="mb-12">
    <p class="font-mono text-[11px] tracking-[0.2em] uppercase text-accent mb-3">
      Concept
    </p>
    <h1 class="font-display font-normal text-fg leading-[0.95] tracking-[-0.02em] text-[38px] sm:text-[52px] lg:text-[64px]">
      Orbit the globe, pan the plane.
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-[16px] sm:text-[17px] leading-[1.6] text-fg-dim max-w-[700px]">
      One set of gestures, interpreted by the active projection. On the
      globe a drag is a real 3D orbit around the planet; on a flat map the
      same drag pans a plane. The cursor stays anchored across a zoom, a
      flick glides with inertia, and a Mapbox-parity API drives the camera
      from code.
    </p>
  </header>

  <article class="space-y-4 text-[15px] sm:text-[16px] leading-[1.65] text-fg-dim">

    <section id="the-model">
      <h2 class="text-[24px] font-medium text-fg leading-[1.2] tracking-[-0.02em] mb-4 pb-3 border-b border-line">
        The model
      </h2>
      <p class="mb-4">
        The camera is <strong class="text-fg">projection-aware</strong>: a
        drag, a scroll, a pinch mean the same thing to the user, but the
        camera resolves them differently depending on what is on screen.
        The 3D globe is a true orbit camera around the planet; the seven
        flat projections move a 2D plane. The input layer reads the active
        projection each frame and routes every gesture down the matching
        path, so the point you grab stays under your finger whether the
        Earth is a sphere or a Mercator sheet.
      </p>
      <p class="mb-4">
        Everything below is true of the engine today, with the one
        not-yet-built part — an <em>animated</em> fly-to — marked as
        roadmap.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="orbit-pan" class="pt-6">
      <h2 class="text-[24px] font-medium text-fg leading-[1.2] tracking-[-0.02em] mb-2">
        Orbit the globe, pan the plane
      </h2>
      <p class="mb-2 max-w-[700px]">
        Pan splits on the projection family: a flat map slides in projected
        metres, the globe rolls in surface degrees.
      </p>

      <FeatureRow num="01" title="Pan the plane">
        On the cylindrical and pseudo-cylindrical projections, a drag
        moves the camera centre in <strong class="text-fg">Web Mercator
        metres</strong>, rotated by the current bearing so a drag tracks
        the screen even on a turned map. The centre wraps across the
        antimeridian and clamps to the Mercator latitude limit
        (±85.051129°) so it can't run off the world.
      </FeatureRow>

      <FeatureRow num="02" title="Orbit the globe">
        In globe mode the view is a real <strong class="text-fg">orbit
        camera</strong>, not a panned plane. A drag rotates the planet
        under the cursor: the grabbed point is found by intersecting the
        pointer ray with the sphere, and the centre rotates so it returns
        under the finger. Because the globe tracks its true centre
        latitude, the orbit rolls <strong class="text-fg">all the way to
        the pole</strong> instead of stopping at the Mercator limit.
      </FeatureRow>
    </section>

    <section id="zoom" class="pt-10">
      <h2 class="text-[24px] font-medium text-fg leading-[1.2] tracking-[-0.02em] mb-2">
        Zoom toward the cursor
      </h2>

      <FeatureRow num="03" title="The point under the cursor stays put">
        Zoom is anchored to the cursor: the geographic point under the
        pointer is unprojected <em>before</em> the zoom, the zoom is
        applied, and the centre is shifted so that same point lands back
        under the cursor. Each projection family uses its own inverse to do
        it — an exact Mercator-metre shift for Web Mercator, a fixed-point
        solve for the other flat forms, the disc inverse for the
        azimuthal discs, and a ray↔sphere solve on the globe — so the spot
        you point at is the spot you zoom into.
      </FeatureRow>

      <FeatureRow num="04" title="Smooth wheel zoom">
        Wheel and trackpad zoom is <strong class="text-fg">eased</strong>,
        not instant: each frame the zoom moves a fraction of the way toward
        a target level, with no spring overshoot, anchored at the cursor
        the whole time. Reversing the wheel mid-glide drops the pending
        overshoot so the direction flips immediately.
      </FeatureRow>
    </section>

    <section id="rotate-tilt" class="pt-10">
      <h2 class="text-[24px] font-medium text-fg leading-[1.2] tracking-[-0.02em] mb-2">
        Rotate and tilt
      </h2>

      <FeatureRow num="05" title="Bearing and pitch, by drag or pinch">
        A right-drag turns the map: horizontal motion changes
        <strong class="text-fg">bearing</strong>, vertical motion changes
        <strong class="text-fg">pitch</strong> (clamped 0–85°). A two-finger
        gesture rotates, pinch-zooms, and tilts at once, and the bearing
        snaps to the nearest 15° on release. On the flat azimuthal discs —
        orthographic, azimuthal-equidistant, stereographic — pitch is
        <strong class="text-fg">locked to 0</strong>, because a 2D disc has
        no meaningful tilt; only the true 3D globe takes a real camera tilt.
      </FeatureRow>
    </section>

    <section id="inertia" class="pt-10">
      <h2 class="text-[24px] font-medium text-fg leading-[1.2] tracking-[-0.02em] mb-2">
        Inertia
      </h2>

      <FeatureRow num="06" title="Flick to glide">
        Release a fast drag and the map keeps moving. The release velocity
        is the cursor's screen-pixel speed, capped so a hard flick can't
        launch the view, and it <strong class="text-fg">decays each
        frame</strong> until it falls below a threshold and stops. A flick
        only starts a glide above a small speed, so a deliberate slow drag
        ends exactly where you let go. The same screen-pixel velocity
        drives the glide for the flat plane, the discs, and the globe.
      </FeatureRow>
    </section>

    <section id="unproject" class="pt-10">
      <h2 class="text-[24px] font-medium text-fg leading-[1.2] tracking-[-0.02em] mb-2">
        Projection-aware unproject
      </h2>

      <FeatureRow num="07" title="Screen pixel → geographic point">
        Turning a screen pixel back into a lon/lat is also
        projection-aware. For the flat projections the pixel is inverted
        through that projection's own inverse — Web Mercator, equirect­angular,
        Natural Earth, and oblique Mercator each recover the correct point on
        Earth, not a one-size Mercator guess. On the globe the pixel is
        unprojected by <strong class="text-fg">intersecting the pointer ray
        with the sphere</strong>, returning null off the limb. This is what
        keeps drag and zoom anchored under the cursor across projections.
      </FeatureRow>
    </section>

    <section id="api" class="pt-10">
      <h2 class="text-[24px] font-medium text-fg leading-[1.2] tracking-[-0.02em] mb-2">
        Programmatic control
      </h2>

      <FeatureRow num="08" title="A Mapbox-parity camera API">
        Drive the camera from code with the familiar methods —
        <code class="font-mono text-fg">setCenter</code>,
        <code class="font-mono text-fg">setZoom</code>,
        <code class="font-mono text-fg">jumpTo</code>,
        <code class="font-mono text-fg">panBy</code>,
        <code class="font-mono text-fg">getBounds</code>,
        <code class="font-mono text-fg">fitBounds</code> — matching MapLibre
        GL JS's signatures. <code class="font-mono text-fg">fitBounds</code>
        fits a lon/lat box by choosing the more constraining of the
        horizontal and vertical fit zooms. Every input is finite-checked and
        clamped (zoom to its min/max, pitch to 0–85°), and
        <code class="font-mono text-fg">setMaxBounds</code> /
        <code class="font-mono text-fg">setMinZoom</code> /
        <code class="font-mono text-fg">setMaxZoom</code> constrain
        interactive gestures too. Drag and <code class="font-mono text-fg">panBy</code>
        are device-pixel-ratio invariant — one CSS pixel of input is the
        same world distance on any display.
      </FeatureRow>
    </section>

    <section id="roadmap" class="pt-10">
      <h2 class="text-[24px] font-medium text-fg leading-[1.2] tracking-[-0.02em] mb-4 pb-3 border-b border-line">
        Where it is going
      </h2>
      <div class="rounded-[8px] bg-bg-card border border-line p-5 sm:p-6 mb-4">
        <p class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-mute mb-3">
          Roadmap · not yet shipped
        </p>
        <p class="mb-4 text-[14px] sm:text-[15px]">
          These are planned, not built. They are on the roadmap, not in the
          engine today:
        </p>
        <ul class="space-y-2.5 text-[14px] sm:text-[15px]">
          <li class="flex items-baseline gap-3">
            <span class="font-mono text-[11px] text-fg-mute pt-0.5 flex-shrink-0">→</span>
            <span><strong class="text-fg">Animated fly-to</strong> — smooth, eased camera
            flights between views. Today <code class="font-mono text-fg">easeTo</code> and
            <code class="font-mono text-fg">flyTo</code> exist for API parity but jump
            instantly to the destination; there is no transition interpolation yet.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-baseline gap-3">
            <span class="font-mono text-[11px] text-fg-mute pt-0.5 flex-shrink-0">→</span>
            <span><strong class="text-fg">Azimuthal-disc unproject</strong> — screen→lon/lat
            for the orthographic / azimuthal-equidistant / stereographic discs is deferred
            (their limb is singular). Drag and pinch on those discs are already anchored via a
            dedicated disc inverse; the general-purpose unproject returns null for them.</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-5 text-[13.5px]">
          Tracked in the repo
          <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/ROADMAP.md`} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-hover transition-colors">ROADMAP.md ↗</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── See it live ── -->
    <section class="border-t border-line pt-12">
      <h2 class="text-[20px] font-medium text-fg leading-[1.2] tracking-[-0.015em] mb-3">
        See it live
      </h2>
      <p class="mb-4">
        The camera is best felt, not read. Drag, scroll, and pinch the
        globe in the
        <a href={`${base}/`} class="text-accent hover:text-accent-hover transition-colors">home page hero</a>,
        or open the
        <a href={`${base}/play`} class="text-accent hover:text-accent-hover transition-colors">playground</a>
        to orbit, pan, and zoom a real map and switch projections under the
        same gestures.
      </p>
    </section>

  </article>

  <SpecLinks specs={[
    { body: 'MapLibre', title: 'MapLibre GL JS — Map camera API (the parity target)', url: 'https://maplibre.org/maplibre-gl-js/docs/API/classes/Map/' },
    { body: 'Mapbox',   title: 'Mapbox GL JS — Map: jumpTo / easeTo / flyTo / fitBounds', url: 'https://docs.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/api/map/' },
    { body: 'W3C',      title: 'Pointer Events — the unified pointer/touch input model', url: 'https://www.w3.org/TR/pointerevents/' },
    { body: 'EPSG',     title: 'EPSG:3857 Web Mercator (the plane a flat pan moves in)', url: 'https://epsg.io/3857' },
  ]} />

  <SeeAlso links={[
    { label: 'Globe & 3D', href: `${base}/docs/concepts/globe`, body: 'The ECEF scene and orbit camera the globe gestures move through.' },
    { label: 'Projection switching', href: `${base}/docs/concepts/projections`, body: 'Why the same input drives the globe and seven flat projections.' },
    { label: 'JavaScript API', href: `${base}/docs/api`, body: '`setCenter`, `jumpTo`, `fitBounds`, `panBy` — the Mapbox-parity camera methods.' },
  ]} />
</Docs>
